## The Problem: Object Reference Equality

React uses **shallow comparison** (`===`) to determine if props changed. When you pass an object as a prop, React compares **references**, not values.

```javascript
// This creates a NEW object on every render, even if values are identical
<MyComponent config={{ theme: 'dark', size: 'large' }} />
```

Every parent render creates a new object literal → new reference → React thinks props changed → child re-renders.

---

## Why This Happens

```javascript
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // ❌ New object reference created on every render
  const config = { theme: 'dark', size: 'large' };

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>
      <Child config={config} /> {/* Re-renders every time count changes */}
    </>
  );
}
```

---

## Solutions

### 1. `useMemo` — Memoize the object
```javascript
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // ✅ Same reference unless dependencies change
  const config = useMemo(() => ({
    theme: 'dark',
    size: 'large'
  }), []); // Empty array = created once

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>
      <Child config={config} />
    </>
  );
}
```

### 2. Move the object outside the component
```javascript
// ✅ Best option if the object doesn't depend on props/state
const CONFIG = { theme: 'dark', size: 'large' };

function Parent() {
  return <Child config={CONFIG} />;
}
```

### 3. `React.memo` — Prevent child re-renders
```javascript
// ✅ Child only re-renders if props actually change (by reference)
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ config }) {
  return <div>{config.theme}</div>;
});
```

### 4. `React.memo` with custom comparator
```javascript
// ✅ Deep comparison when you need value equality
const Child = React.memo(
  function Child({ config }) {
    return <div>{config.theme}</div>;
  },
  (prevProps, nextProps) => {
    // Return true if props are equal (skip re-render)
    return prevProps.config.theme === nextProps.config.theme &&
           prevProps.config.size === nextProps.config.size;
  }
);
```

### 5. Pass primitive values instead
```javascript
// ✅ Primitives compare by value, not reference
<Child theme="dark" size="large" />
```

---

## Decision Guide

| Situation | Solution |
|-----------|----------|
| Object doesn't depend on state/props | Move outside component |
| Object depends on state/props | `useMemo` |
| Child is expensive to render | `React.memo` |
| Need deep equality check | `React.memo` + custom comparator |
| Can restructure props | Pass primitives |

---

## Key Takeaway

> **`React.memo` alone won't help** if you're passing a new object reference each render — the memo comparison will always see "changed" props. You need to stabilize the reference with `useMemo` or by moving the object outside the component.

The most common fix is combining both: `useMemo` to stabilize the reference + `React.memo` to prevent unnecessary re-renders.
